  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 316 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nOn Monday, we land in a narrow region of ridging as another system \nbegins to deepen across the northern Plains. Clear skies and light \nwinds dominantly out of the southeast will help us continue on our \nslow and steady warming trend. The whole CWA should reach the 80s \nwith the western counties potentially breaching 90. It will be a \nvery pleasant day, especially after some of the blast furnace days \nwe have endured as of late. Highs are largely back to normal Tuesday \nwith more clear skies and light winds.\n\nBy Wednesday night, the aforementioned upper wave forming over the \nnorthern Plains sends some energy down our way, bringing rain \nchances currently around 20-40% to areas north of I-44. Though a \nlong-term severe risk has not been identified (rightfully so, as \nthis is Day 5), some global models are indicating the potential for \n1-2k J/Kg of MUCAPE with this activity. This system will be \nmonitored over the coming days to assess its capability for any \nfunny business. It does not appear that we will see a true frontal \npassage with this system, so highs remain in the 90s through the \nrest of the forecast period.\n\nIn the 8-14 day timeframe, the CPC has included our area as having a \n40-60% chance of experiencing extreme heat next weekend (8/8-9), \nalong with a truly expansive swath of the country, from the Gulf all \nthe way to the east coast. Far southwest Missouri and eastern Kansas \nare also identified as at risk for rapid onset drought in the 8-14 \nday outlook.",
